+Dynamic and Deformable Fields extend static radiance field representations to model scenes that change over time, either through rigid camera motion, non-rigid object deformation, or temporal dynamics. Unlike static methods that assume a fixed geometry, these methods introduce a time dimension $t$ and a mechanism to map observed coordinates to a canonical space, enabling the synthesis of novel views for dynamic content. They are distinguished from sibling architectures like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) or [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md) by their explicit handling of temporal deformation fields or displacement vectors, rather than relying solely on static appearance embeddings or sinusoidal basis functions for time.
 
